 watson  that you would want him to answer  you know that i haven't thought about  that because he hasn't really directly  answered anything and we also haven't  been able to ask you many football  questions right so  he has maintained his innocence and he's  been direct in doing that now the ruling  obviously is in conflict with that and  some of it was out there but  i just go back into this trade was made  you know on friday in mid-march the nfl  owners meetings were a couple of weeks  later  like this was construed to us  off the record and you know just in ways  things go as  as this was kind of gonna be the outcome  that desean watson made some mistakes  but didn't do  uh anything  you know really over the top and that  the team was going to swallow this  there was a sense of desperation here  obviously when you and i last talked it  was pretty clear they were going to  break up with baker mayfield you're  never getting this player if these  circumstances aren't out there right so  they took this all on and right now  it feels like a win  this is what i don't understand we're  talking to zach jackson he uh covers the  browns nfl writer for the athletic  has anybody talked about therapy for  deshaun watson because when the judge  talks about a pattern of behavior that's  egregious and the behavior was  non-violent sexual conduct with multiple  women here if you're the cleveland  browns  moving forward you feel pretty confident  that desean watson is all of a sudden  going to learn his lesson  yeah no i think there was always a level  of trust in the grounds and there had to  be  you know this isn't deciding which tight  end you're going to sign in free agency  this was taking on this player and  giving him this contract and signing up  for all this  the therapy counseling thing was  broached at the initial press conference  multiple times by people with desean and  his answer was his standard maintain the  innocence  you know and the quote that's kind of  linge